--swiftversion {{SWIFT_VERSION}}
--exclude DerivedData,DerivedData-*,.build,.swiftpm,**/Generated
--indent 4
--indentcase false
--trimwhitespace always
--voidtype void
--maxwidth 120
--wraparguments before-first
--wrapparameters before-first
--wrapcollections before-first
--closingparen balanced
--funcattributes prev-line
--typeattributes prev-line
--organize-types class,struct,enum,actor,extension

# organizeDeclarations is what makes type_contents_order fixable. SwiftLint's
# type_contents_order rule has no autocorrect, and across this fleet it produced
# 55% of ALL lint violations — 371 in one app, 117 in another — every one of them
# a member that had to be moved by hand. A rule with no fixer is a rule that
# accumulates.
#
# `--organization-mode type` is the load-bearing flag. The DEFAULT mode is
# `visibility`, which groups public/internal/private instead of by declaration
# kind, and clears almost nothing: measured on one app, default mode took
# type_contents_order from 117 to 80, while type mode with the order below took
# it to 1.
#
# --type-order mirrors .swiftlint.yml's type_contents_order. The two lists are
# one decision: change either and they drift, and the formatter starts producing
# layouts the linter rejects.
#
# --mark-categories false: inserting MARK comments would be a correct-but-huge
# diff across every existing file, and it is a separate opinion from ordering.
#
# THREE CORRECTIONS found by onboarding kit, where 48 type_contents_order
# violations survived the formatter:
#   * `extension` was missing from --organize-types. Nearly every surviving
#     violation lived in an extension body, so the rule that exists to make
#     type_contents_order fixable never ran on the code that needed it.
#   * swiftUIMethod sat BEFORE staticMethod. SwiftLint has no swiftUIMethod
#     concept — it sees `private func body() -> some View` as other_method, which
#     must follow type_method. Any SwiftUI type with a static helper was
#     therefore guaranteed a violation no formatter pass could clear.
#   * --before-marks typealias: without it the formatter actively REVERTED the
#     correct order for nested typealiases, fighting the linter every run.
--enable organizeDeclarations
--organization-mode type
--type-order beforeMarks,nestedType,staticProperty,staticPropertyWithBody,classPropertyWithBody,overriddenProperty,instanceProperty,swiftUIPropertyWrapper,swiftUIProperty,instancePropertyWithBody,instanceLifecycle,staticMethod,classMethod,swiftUIMethod,instanceMethod
--before-marks typealias
--mark-categories false
--extensionacl on-declarations

--enable blankLinesBetweenScopes
--enable consecutiveSpaces
--enable duplicateImports
--enable redundantSelf
--enable sortImports
--enable trailingCommas

# wrapIfExpressionBodies expands a one-line if-expression
#
#     let ok = if case .resolved = model.phase { true } else { false }
#
# into five lines. That is a formatting opinion the LINTER then charges for:
# type_body_length counts real code lines (it already excludes comments and
# whitespace), so a type using if-expressions grows toward the 300/350
# thresholds purely because the formatter reshaped it.
#
# Measured on underfoot: the rule fired 43 times in one test file, taking it
# from 334 to 378 lines and pushing its type body from under 300 to 318 — a
# type_body_length error that did not exist before formatting. The formatter
# produced code the linter rejects, which is the one thing these two configs
# must never do to each other.
#
# With it disabled, that repo reaches `swiftformat --lint` exit 0 AND
# `swiftlint --strict` 0 violations across 166 files. The one-line form is also
# the idiomatic Swift 5.9 if-expression style.
--disable wrapIfExpressionBodies

# wrapIfStatementBodies is the same defect one rule over, found the same way —
# by a repo going red after a format pass that was supposed to make it compliant.
#
#     set: { presented in if !presented { ravelryEntry?.cancel() } },
#
# becomes
#
#     set: {
#         presented in if !presented {
#             ravelryEntry?.cancel()
#         }
#     },
#
# and SwiftLint's closure_parameter_position rejects that, because `presented in`
# is no longer adjacent to the opening brace. The formatter wrapped the closure
# to fit the expanded body and did not hoist the parameter list with it.
#
# Measured on skein: formatting with this rule ON took SwiftLint from 0
# violations to 5 (two closure_parameter_position, plus type_body_length,
# file_length and closure_body_length from the added lines). With it off, the
# same pass reaches swiftformat --lint 0 AND swiftlint --strict 0 across 340
# files. Confirmed it changes nothing for underfoot, which was already clean.

--disable acronyms
--disable modifierOrder
--disable swiftTestingTestCaseNames

# SwiftFormat 0.62.0 (2026-07-06) shipped several semantic auto-rewrite rules
# enabled by default (isEmpty, preferContains, preferFirstWhere, preferCountWhere,
# preferMinOverSorted, preferFlatMap) that duplicate SwiftLint opt-in rules this
# config already enables (empty_count, contains_over_filter_count) — two tools
# independently deciding the same rewrite, one silently, one as a warning a
# developer can judge. 0.62.1 (the next day) walked all of these back to
# disabled-by-default, but Homebrew's Brewfile syntax has no clean way to pin
# swiftformat/swiftlint to an exact version (no versioned formulae exist for
# either), so a future release re-enabling any of these by default would land
# in every project's CI with no review. Disable them explicitly so behavior is
# deterministic regardless of which SwiftFormat version runs, not just the
# current one. SwiftLint owns this class of decision going forward — if a
# corresponding SwiftLint opt-in rule is ever added for one of these, keep the
# SwiftFormat side disabled.
--disable isEmpty
--disable preferContains
--disable preferFirstWhere
--disable preferCountWhere
--disable preferMinOverSorted
--disable preferFlatMap

--disable wrapIfStatementBodies
